4. SENSOR INTERFACE
4.1 The Input Alphabet Σ
Definition 3 (Input Alphabet). Σ is the set of all distinguishable transitions the agent can
observe:
```
Σ = { σ : σ is a valid sensor transition }
```
Since each sensor has finite domain D_i, and the transition is a change from one value to
another, the total alphabet size is bounded by:
```
|Σ| ≤ ∏_i |D_i|²
```
This is finite but astronomical. In practice, the alphabet is restricted by:
· Rate limits — only transitions within the sampling window count.
· Relevance filters — only transitions relevant to active tiles are admitted.
· Hysteresis — small changes below a declared threshold are ignored.
These filters are declared per deployment, not per standard.
4.2 The Sampling Discipline
Rule: sensors are sampled at declared rates. Sampling is synchronous with the FSM cycle.
No asynchronous interrupts admit state changes.
This is a departure from typical real-time design, and it is deliberate. Asynchronous inputs
violate the determinism requirement of the FSM. By sampling synchronously, the state
transition is always s' = δ(s, σ) where σ is the sampled input for that cycle. No race
conditions. No nondeterministic interleaving.
4.3 The Transition Function Definition 4 (Transition). δ : S × Σ → S. Given a state and a sampled input, the next state is
uniquely determined.
```
s_{t+1} = δ(s_t, σ_t)
```
Properties:
· Deterministic — δ is a function, not a relation.
· Total — δ is defined for every (s, σ) ∈ S × Σ.
· Bounded — every transition completes within the FSM cycle budget.
These three properties are what the Verifiable Agency Theorem requires (Definition 5 in the
proof).
