6. INVARIANTS
6.1 Definition
Definition 5 (Invariant Set). I ⊆ S is the set of states the agent may occupy. Every state
transition must preserve I:
```
∀ s ∈ I, σ ∈ Σ : δ(s, σ) ∈ I
```
6.2 The Invariant Catalog
Each deployment declares its invariant set. Examples:
Invariant Domain Meaning
I_safe Automotive Brake pressure never exceeds safe bounds
I_bounded All Cycle counter never exceeds T_max
I_no_overflow All All integer fields stay in declared ranges
I_envelope All No action is committed outside its envelope
Rule: invariants are declared in ROM and checked by VERIFY. A transition that violates an
invariant is refused with 0x-UNSAFE-ACT.
6.3 Invariant Verification
The invariant set I is finite (subset of finite S). Therefore I can be exhaustively verified by
finite model-checking. This is Corollary 2 of the Verifiable Agency Theorem, applied to state. 7. STATE AND THE THREE FIXES
Fix How A4 Contributes
Fix 1 (Architecture) A4 fixes the shape of state. Two engineers following A4 produce
structurally identical state models.
Fix 2 (Behavior) A4 leaves the content of D_i, invariants, and sensor schemas to
deployment. Different domains, same grammar.
Fix 3 (Safety) A4 makes state finite and deterministic. Without this, the Verifiable Agency
Theorem cannot be established. A4 is a necessary condition for Fix 3.
