10. CONCLUSION
A4 defines the state representation and world interface that the agentic extension requires.
Without it, the Verifiable Agency Theorem has no premises, VERIFY has no object, and the
append-only log has no content.
The three commitments:
1. State is finite. By construction, not by hope.
2. State is deterministic. Transitions are functions, not relations.
3. State is total. Faults are values, not absences.
These commitments are what make the rest of the agentic extension possible. A3 (plan
representation) sits on top of A4. A5 (VERIFY) checks predicates over A4 state. A7
(memory) logs A4 transitions.
A4 is the foundation. Everything else is downstream. APPENDIX A — Plain Language
What is the state of an AI agent?
Everything it knows about itself and the world, right now, in one place.
Why does it have to be finite?
So you can prove what it will do next.
How do you make it finite?
You quantize the world. Sensors have a fixed set of values. Numbers have bounds. Nothing
is infinite.
But the world is infinite.
The world is. The agent's model of the world is not. The model is a finite projection —
enough for the task, and no more.
Is that safe?
Yes — because the model is what the agent acts on. If the model is finite and the transitions
are deterministic, the agent's behavior is provable.
And the guarantee?
The agent cannot be uncertain about its own state. Uncertainty is not representable.
What is this called?
A4 — the state and world model.
